-- title:
--   Steinitz's theorem on primitive elements
-- statement:
--   **Steinitz's theorem on primitive elements.** A field extension $E/F$ is algebraic and generated by a single element $\alpha$, that is $E=F(\alpha)$, if and only if it has only finitely many intermediate fields.
--
--   This is the sharp form of the primitive element theorem. It shows, for example, that every finite separable extension is simple, since it has finitely many intermediate fields by Galois theory. It also shows that the extension $\mathbb F_p(s,t)/\mathbb F_p(s^p,t^p)$ is not simple.
--
--   **Formalization note.** Mathlib's `Field.exists_primitive_element_iff_finite_intermediateField`. `IntermediateField.adjoin F {α}` is $F(\alpha)$ and `⊤` is $E$.
-- source:
--   Listed in Mathlib's curated theorem manifests (docs/1000.yaml); formalized in Mathlib as `Field.exists_primitive_element_iff_finite_intermediateField`. Proof here reduces to that Mathlib result.

import Mathlib

namespace FamousTheorems

theorem steinitz_primitive_element_theorem (F E : Type*) [Field F] [Field E] [Algebra F E] :
    (Algebra.IsAlgebraic F E ∧ ∃ α : E, IntermediateField.adjoin F {α} = ⊤) ↔ Finite (IntermediateField F E) := by sorry

end FamousTheorems
